From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bastiaan van der Plaat <bastiaan.v.d.plaat@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:10:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add DS sound

Instead of mixing Quake's channels in software, snd_nds.c plays every
channel on a hardware channel of its own, which does the resampling,
volume and stereo panning: the ambient and entity channels on hardware
channels 0-11, the four loudest static sounds (torches, machines) on
12-15. The samples play straight from the sound cache, which Quake keeps
8-bit signed or 16-bit at 11025 Hz. Only the sounds that play stay in
the sound cache, the ambient sounds only near water or sky: with all the static sounds of a level in it, it pushed
out the models every frame, the start map ran at 2 fps. Sounds load when
they first play instead of with the level, all of them pushed its models
out of the cache while it loaded, and a sound that doesn't fit without
pushing out what was just used waits a second, one loaded again within
two seconds, which pushes out models drawn every frame or is pushed out
by them, ten seconds. The software mixer isn't
built, there is no DMA buffer, S_ClearBuffer stops what the driver
plays instead, so no old sound loops during loading. When the cache
frees or moves samples that play, their hardware channel stops before
it plays what comes there next.
---
 WinQuake/snd_dma.c |   6 +-
 WinQuake/snd_nds.c | 341 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 WinQuake/sound.h   |   3 +
 3 files changed, 349 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 WinQuake/snd_nds.c

diff --git a/WinQuake/snd_dma.c b/WinQuake/snd_dma.c
index a261f12..6df279d 100644
--- a/WinQuake/snd_dma.c
+++ b/WinQuake/snd_dma.c
@@ -206,7 +206,6 @@ void S_Init (void)
 
 	S_Startup ();
 
-	SND_InitScaletable ();
 
 	known_sfx = Hunk_AllocName (MAX_SFX*sizeof(sfx_t), "sfx_t");
 	num_sfx = 0;
@@ -563,6 +562,11 @@ void S_StopAllSoundsC (void)
 void S_ClearBuffer (void)
 {
 	int		clear;
+
+	// the DS driver plays from the sound cache, without a buffer to clear,
+	// it stops what plays
+	if (sound_started && shm)
+		SNDDMA_ClearBuffer ();
 		
 #ifdef _WIN32
 	if (!sound_started || !shm || (!shm->buffer && !pDSBuf))
